statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=3$ is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ we must have that $\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5,$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{nullity}(T)>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(T)=3$; $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 true] [17592186326728 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=3$ is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ we must have that $\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5,$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{nullity}(T)>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(T)=3$; $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 false] [17592186363238 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=6$ is false. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ and by the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not invertible. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $T$ is not invertible, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6,$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ is true. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim(Ker}(T))=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 true] [17592186363238 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=6$ is false. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ and by the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not invertible. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $T$ is not invertible, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6,$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ is true. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim(Ker}(T))=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 false] [17592186364213 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$ is true. Since the columns of $T$ satisfies ${v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=0$, then they are linearly dependent, which gives us $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Therefore, according to the Invertible Matrix Theorem, we must have $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=3$ is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the transformation $T$ can't be a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 true] [17592186364213 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$ is true. Since the columns of $T$ satisfies ${v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=0$, then they are linearly dependent, which gives us $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Therefore, according to the Invertible Matrix Theorem, we must have $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=3$ is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the transformation $T$ can't be a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 false] [17592186366039 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$” is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement “If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$” is true. Since $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of $Tx=0$, we have that $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and therefore $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “If $T(x)=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has no solution then $\\text{rank}(T)=3$” is false. Since $T(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has no solution, we have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Hence, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is “If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$” only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 true] [17592186366039 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$” is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement “If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$” is true. Since $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of $Tx=0$, we have that $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and therefore $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “If $T(x)=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has no solution then $\\text{rank}(T)=3$” is false. Since $T(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has no solution, we have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Hence, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is “If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$” only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 false] [17592186366097 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{n}$ into ${R}^{n},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={⟨x,y,z⟩\\,|\\,x+y=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)+1=n$” is false. Solving $x+y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-y \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+2 & =n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If there exists a vector $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ then $|T|\\ne 0$” is false. If $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$ then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{n}$, so, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not a bijection. Thus, the matrix $T$ is not invertible, and, consequently, $|T|=0.$\n\n- Statement “If ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” is false. Since $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$, then ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$ But the vectors are linearly independent, therefore, the dimension of  $\\text{Ker}(T)$ is at least $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139537082 17592186576411 true] [17592186366097 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{n}$ into ${R}^{n},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={⟨x,y,z⟩\\,|\\,x+y=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)+1=n$” is false. Solving $x+y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-y \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+2 & =n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If there exists a vector $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ then $|T|\\ne 0$” is false. If $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$ then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{n}$, so, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not a bijection. Thus, the matrix $T$ is not invertible, and, consequently, $|T|=0.$\n\n- Statement “If ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” is false. Since $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$, then ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$ But the vectors are linearly independent, therefore, the dimension of  $\\text{Ker}(T)$ is at least $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139537082 17592186576411 false] [17592186366427 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$; $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 true] [17592186366427 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$; $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 false] [17592186406841 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” is true. Since $y=z=0,$ any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $Tx=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has more than one solution, then $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$” is false. Since $Tx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has more than one solution, there are ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}$ such that  ${u}_{1}\\ne {u}_{2}$ and \n\n$$\nT({u}_{1})=T({u}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means, $T$ is not a bijection and according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$, then $T$ is invertible” is false. Notice that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 true] [17592186406841 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” is true. Since $y=z=0,$ any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $Tx=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has more than one solution, then $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$” is false. Since $Tx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has more than one solution, there are ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}$ such that  ${u}_{1}\\ne {u}_{2}$ and \n\n$$\nT({u}_{1})=T({u}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means, $T$ is not a bijection and according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$, then $T$ is invertible” is false. Notice that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 false] [17592186406843 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=4$ is true. Indeed, since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=4$ is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is $\\text{rank}(T)=4$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 true] [17592186406843 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=4$ is true. Indeed, since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=4$ is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is $\\text{rank}(T)=4$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 false] [17592186417703 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that  $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 true] [17592186417703 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that  $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 false] [17592186417704 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ is false. Since $T$ is a linear transformation and $T({u}_{1})-T({u}_{2})=0,$ we have $T({u}_{1}-{u}_{2})=0.$ So ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$ is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3,$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 true] [17592186417704 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ is false. Since $T$ is a linear transformation and $T({u}_{1})-T({u}_{2})=0,$ we have $T({u}_{1}-{u}_{2})=0.$ So ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$ is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3,$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$ only." 13194139537082 17592186576411 false]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
